UNIT lint_transcription
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs transcriptionLint with its CODE_SHAPES table and stripQuoted, reading lines
  through sir-parse.mjs sirLines (the parser's own line reader) and quoted spans through sir-parse.mjs quotedSpans — the deterministic
  floor of SIR_SCHEMA's "a primary is a contract, not a transcription" lint; reproduced exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG lint_transcription(text: text) -> json
ENVELOPE text only: any well-formed text, empty included, any line-break kinds, non-ASCII and astral characters included.
  Non-text input is outside the envelope (the package throws a type error on its first operation; no vector).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Measure how code-shaped a primary text is. The text is cut into lines; each line, after its double-quoted
  literals are blanked, is tested against thirteen fixed code shapes. The result is a map with five entries, in this order:
  hits (a list of maps with entries line, what, text: one per shape that matched on a line), hitLines (how many distinct
  lines have at least one hit), lines (how many lines are non-blank), commentLines (how many lines start with "#" once
  trimmed) and density (hitLines divided by lines, or 0 when lines is 0). Every count is a number. The 10% gate is applied by the
  caller (pack, the validator) to density with a strict greater-than; it is not part of this unit.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Lines. Scanning left to right, a BREAK is a CR (000D) immediately followed by a line feed (000A) — the pair is ONE break
  — or else a lone CR, or a lone line feed. The text is split at every BREAK and nowhere else: LINE SEPARATOR (2028),
  PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR (2029), NEXT LINE (0085), form feed and vertical tab stay inside their line. Lines are numbered from 1.
  A text of k breaks has k + 1 lines, so the empty text is one empty line, a trailing break adds an empty last line, and
  CR CR LF is two breaks (a lone CR, then a pair).
  Normalization, applied to every line before anything else reads it, in this order: first, the line's LEAD is its
  maximal leading run of spaces (0020), tabs (0009) and BYTE ORDER MARKs (FEFF), in any mix, possibly empty; every FEFF of
  the LEAD is removed and its spaces and tabs are kept in their order (so space, FEFF, space, FEFF, x becomes space, space, x;
  a FEFF after any other character stays, and one pass is final: the lead left behind holds no FEFF); then every space and
  tab at the very end of the line is removed (a run of them; any other
  trailing WS character, a no-break space or a FEFF for one, stays). Every
  later rule reads the NORMALIZED line, and its "end of the line" is the normalized end: a trailing space or tab is never
  seen by any shape, while a trailing no-break space is an ordinary WS character of the line.
R2 Stripping. Each line is copied into its STRIPPED form, in which every QUOTED span is replaced by the two characters
  of an empty quoted literal (a pair of double quotes). Spans are found left to right. A double quote OPENS a span only
  (a) at a TOKEN START: it is the first character of the line, or the character just before it in the line is one of
  space, tab, "(", "[", "{", ",", "=", ":", ";", "|", "<", ">", "!", "&", "-", "~" (16 OPENERS; any other
  neighbour opens nothing: a letter, a digit, "+", "*", "?", "/", ")", "]", "}", ".", a single quote, a no-break space, a
  FEFF); or
  (b) directly after a span: it is the character right after the closing quote of the span just found, so two literals
  written side by side are two spans. A double quote that follows a quote which closed nothing (a quote that never opened)
  opens nothing by (b), and a quote inside an earlier span is never considered. From an opening quote the span is that
  quote, a sequence of items, and the first double quote that ends the sequence. An item is a backslash together with the
  ONE character after it, whatever it is (a double quote, a backslash, LS, PS, an astral character), or one character other
  than a double quote or a backslash. When the line ends first (a backslash that is the last character included) the quote
  is UNCLOSED: it opens no span, and no later quote of the line opens one.
  Single quotes and backticks never open a span. Only the shape tests (R3) read the stripped form; R6's text is the
  normalized line, unstripped.
R3 Shapes. Each row of TABLE SHAPES is tested once against the stripped line; a row matches when its condition holds
  anywhere in the line (only the narration row is tied to the line start). Every matching row is one hit. Letters are
  case-sensitive except in the narration row. A line may yield several hits; a row never yields two hits on one line.
TABLE SHAPES — what | condition on the stripped line S. "bounded" means a BOUNDARY just before and just after the word
  "typeof"                     | the word "typeof", bounded
  "arrow fn"                   | the two characters "=>" side by side
  "method call"                | a full stop, then one of "match" "reduce" "slice" "test" "exec" "split" "map" "filter", then "(" — nothing between them
  "JS builtin"                 | a BOUNDARY, one of "parseInt" "parseFloat" "Number" "String" "Math", then zero or more WS characters, then a full stop or "("
  "if ("                       | a BOUNDARY, the word "if", zero or more WS characters, then "("
  "return"                     | the word "return", bounded
  "loop"                       | a BOUNDARY then "for", one or more WS, one or more WORD, one or more WS, then "in" followed by a BOUNDARY; or a BOUNDARY then "for" or "while", zero or more WS, then "("
  "STEP/NODE/BRANCH narration" | S begins with zero or more WS, at most one "#", zero or more WS, one of "STEP" "NODE" "BRANCH" "GATE" in any ASCII letter case, one or more WS, then a WORD character or a hyphen
  "indexed assignment"         | a WORD character, "[", one or more WORD characters, "]", zero or more WS, then OP, then an equals sign whose next character, if any, is neither an equals sign nor ">"; OP is nothing or exactly one of "**" "<<" ">>" ">>>" "&&" "||" "??" "-" "+" "*" "/" "%" "&" "|" "^" written with no WS inside or before the equals sign (an equals sign ending S qualifies; "==" and "=>" never do at that sign; "<=", ">=", "!=" and "?=" are not OP forms)
  "dialect regex literal"      | some occurrence of the pair "/^" (any, not only the first) and, later in the line and not overlapping it, the pair "$/", with no LT character between them
  "JS value name (say none)"   | the word "null" or the word "undefined", bounded
  "assignment statement"       | ASSIGN (R7)
  "capture-group reference"    | a BOUNDARY, the word "group", exactly one ASCII digit, then a BOUNDARY
R4 A hit is the map (line = the line's number, what = the row's what text exactly as in the table, text = R6). The hits
  list is ordered by line number, and within a line by the row order of TABLE SHAPES.
R5 Counts. A line is BLANK when it is empty or consists only of WS characters. The entry lines is the number of non-BLANK lines.
  The entry commentLines is the number of lines whose first non-WS character is "#" (a comment line is non-BLANK and counts
  in lines too). The entry hitLines is the number of distinct line numbers among the hits. The entry density is hitLines / lines as a double (correctly
  rounded division, not rounded further: 1 of 3 is 0.3333333333333333, 1 of 10 is exactly the double nearest 0.1, the same
  double as 3 of 30), and 0 when lines is 0. A BLANK line never has a hit, so density never exceeds 1.
R6 The text of a hit is the normalized line (R1, before R2) with every leading and trailing WS character removed, then cut to
  its first 96 characters (code points: a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane counts as ONE and is never split);
  a shorter text is kept whole.
R7 ASSIGN holds on S when there is an equals sign E in S such that all of these hold:
  (a) skipping back from E over zero or more WS characters reaches a run of WORD characters (the whole maximal run) that holds
  at least one ASCII letter or underscore — so "a1 = b;" qualifies and "1 = 2;" does not;
  (b) after E come zero or more WS characters and then a character C that is neither an equals sign nor WS — so a double
  equals sign or a compound operator such as "+=" never qualifies at that sign;
  (c) the last non-WS character of S is a semicolon standing strictly after C, with no LT character between C and it.
PINNED charset WS = the code points 0009 000A 000B 000C 000D 0020 00A0 1680 2000 to 200A 2028 2029 202F 205F 3000 FEFF (hexadecimal, 25 members) CITED ECMA-262 WhiteSpace and LineTerminator, the host's white-space class and trim set; probe over every BMP code unit: the class and the trim set are both exactly these 25; 0085 (NEXT LINE) and 180E are NOT members, FEFF is — this is NOT the Unicode White_Space property
PINNED charset WORD = the ASCII letters A to Z and a to z, the ASCII digits 0 to 9, and the underscore CITED ECMA-262 non-unicode word class; probe: a lone accented letter is not WORD, so "éreturn" has a BOUNDARY before "return" and is a hit
PINNED charset LT = the code points 2028 2029 (hexadecimal) CITED ECMA-262 LineTerminator minus the CR and line feed that R1 already consumed as breaks; the "any character" of the package's patterns crosses neither, so "x = 1" 2028 ";" is not ASSIGN while "x = 1;" 2028 is
PINNED grammar BOUNDARY = a position between two characters, or at either end of S, where exactly one of the two neighbours is a WORD character; outside S counts as non-WORD CITED ECMA-262 word-boundary assertion over WORD
INVARIANT 0 <= hitLines <= lines, 0 <= commentLines <= lines, 0 <= density <= 1, and hitLines <= the length of hits.
INVARIANT the result depends on each line alone: reordering lines reorders hits and leaves every count unchanged.
ORACLE value -> oracles/lint_transcription.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 cc16f0acb0b6fb233fa86c96c89868cc8de68cb70a1560835f6ff3b05fb5067c
SCOPE IN: R1-R7 over any text in ENVELOPE. OUT: the 10% gate and its percent message (lintFamily), the language-leak and
  pin lints, the Jev dial score, and non-text input.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (rederive, the trust kernel's own SIR v0.5 lab core), cli/sir/v05.mjs: transcriptionLint,
  CODE_SHAPES, stripQuoted; cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs: sirLines, quotedSpans. Cut cold 2026-10-26 for REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN Tier 1; round 2
  followed the kernel fix (line reader and code-point cut) at b15b56613f9d; round 3 follows FINDINGS #50 at 31803f408bef
  (the indexed-assignment row needs no right-hand side); round 4 follows FINDINGS #59 #60 #68 at 978444f3baf3 (quoted spans
  open only at a token start and escape any one character; the indexed-assignment row takes the compound operators and
  refuses "=>"); round 5 follows FINDINGS #75 #76 at bb8ded84fa50 (the 19 openers, a quote right after a closed span opens
  the next, and a FEFF run after the indent is dropped); round 5b follows FINDINGS #99 #107 at 6199e2cc8b75 (every FEFF
  of the leading space/tab/mark run is dropped; "+", "*" and "?" are no longer openers).
